  "Question": "Walking 12 kilometers takes the same amount of time as cycling 36 kilometers. The cycling speed is 8 kilometers per hour faster than the walking speed. Let the walking speed be \\(x\\) kilometers per hour. Which equation models this situation?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{12}{x}=\\frac{36}{x+8}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{12}{x+8}=\\frac{36}{x}\\)",
    "C": "\\(12x=36(x+8)\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The walking time is \\(\\frac{12}{x}\\). The cycling speed is \\(x+8\\), and the cycling time is \\(\\frac{36}{x+8}\\). Equating the times gives \\(\\frac{12}{x}=\\frac{36}{x+8}\\).",
